{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-970ac5a75c9f5e45","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n3\tepic_count: 7\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9020\n10\t\n11\tAMD GPU (ROCm) enablement for serving and training, decomposed into a\n12\t7-epic DAG. The strategy's core shape: a hardware validation campaign\n13\t(Investigation) gates serving productization; an upstream image-cadence\n14\tassessment (Investigation) gates the image-strategy-dependent\n15\timplementations; Kueue quota handling is independent config work;\n16\tdocumentation lands last.\n17\t\n18\t## Epic List\n19\t\n20\t| ID | Title | Type | Component | Team | Priority |\n21\t|----|-------|------|-----------|------|----------|\n22\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | AMD Instinct validation campaign for vLLM ROCm serving | Investigation | Serving Runtimes | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 | Upstream ROCm image cadence assessment and base-image strategy | Investigation | Serving Runtimes | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 | Productize supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime | Implementation | Serving Runtimes | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 | Konflux build pipeline for the vLLM ROCm image variant | Implementation | Serving Runtimes | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 | Kueue quota handling for amd.com/gpu | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E006 | PyTorch ROCm training support with Training Operator | Implementation | Training Operator | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n28\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E007 | Document AMD GPU (ROCm) support | Implementation | Documentation | RHOAI Serving | P1 |\n29\t\n30\t## Dependency DAG\n31\t\n32\t```mermaid\n33\tgraph TD\n34\t    E001[\"E001 validation campaign (Inv)\"] --> E003[\"E003 vLLM ROCm productization\"]\n35\t    E002[\"E002 ROCm cadence assessment (Inv)\"] --> E003\n36\t    E002 --> E006[\"E006 PyTorch ROCm training\"]\n37\t    E003 --> E007[\"E007 docs\"]\n38\t    E004[\"E004 Konflux ROCm pipeline\"] --> E007\n39\t    E005[\"E005 Kueue amd.com/gpu quota\"] --> E007\n40\t    E006 --> E007\n41\t```\n42\t\n43\tRoots (start immediately): E001, E002, E004, E005. Critical path:\n44\tE001 → E003 → E007 (length 3).\n45\t\n46\t## DAG Justification\n47\t\n48\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n49\t|------|------|-----------|\n50\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates) | The campaign's go/no-go outcome determines which models the productization epic covers — a true gate (`rewrite`: scope changes with the cleared model list; if nothing clears, productization is descoped). |\n51\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates) | The cadence assessment selects the base-image strategy (pin upstream ROCm images vs build-from-source with patches), which changes the productization approach — true gate (`rewrite`). |\n52\t| E002 → E006 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates) | The same assessment determines the PyTorch ROCm worker-image strategy for training — true gate (`rewrite`). |\n53\t| E003 → E007 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes the productized serving runtime and its validated model list. |\n54\t| E004 → E007 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation reflects the released image/pipeline state. |\n55\t| E005 → E007 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation covers amd.com/gpu quota usage. |\n56\t| E006 → E007 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation covers the supported training path. |\n57\t\n58\tDeliberate **non-edges** (parallelism):\n59\t\n60\t| Pair | Rule | Rationale |\n61\t|------|------|-----------|\n62\t| E004 ∥ E003 | Rule 10 | Konflux onboarding and the general implementation must run in parallel. The coupling (\"ROCm image variant builds green in the midstream pipeline\") is an acceptance-criterion gate on E003, never a DAG edge. |\n63\t| E004 ∥ E002/E001 | Rules 8/10 spirit | Pipeline config is independent of the investigations' outcomes; the base image it references is a configuration reference resolved at execution time (AC, not edge). |\n64\t| E005 ∥ E006 | Rules 14/15 + Rule 13 carve-out | Different repos/components with no shared build artifact. Kueue quota configuration is a configuration reference for training jobs — the end-to-end admission check is an AC on E006, not an edge. |\n65\t| E001 ∥ E002 | Rule 14 | Independent investigations: the campaign uses upstream ROCm builds as-is; the cadence question only matters for productized release alignment. |\n66\t| Serving stream ∥ training stream | Rule 14 | Different components, repos, and teams; the serving go/no-go does not change what the training epics do. |\n67\t\n68\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n69\t\n70\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Coverage notes |\n71\t|-----|----------|---------|----------------|\n72\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E001 | Validation campaign as an Investigation: its deliverable is the parity data plus the go/no-go scope that gates productization. |\n73\t| HLR-2 | P0 | E003, E004 | E004 is the Konflux pipeline enabler for the new ROCm image variant in the already-onboarded midstream repo (Rule 20, new container image in existing repo — repo-onboarding skipped); no DAG edge to E003 (Rule 10). |\n74\t| HLR-3 | P1 | E005, E006 | Split along the component boundary (Distributed Workloads quota handling vs Training Operator job support), not by priority — both epics are required for the same HLR and are not independently deferrable. |\n75\t| HLR-4 | P2 | E007 | `docs-authoring` epic; priority P1 via the docs-authoring exception (strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1), not P2. |\n76\t\n77\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. No epic maps to HLRs at\n78\tmultiple priority levels, so no priority-split or bundling analysis applies.\n79\t\n80\t## Health Warnings\n81\t\n82\t1. **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy omits the Impacted Teams table,\n83\t   the Dependencies table, Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and NFRs (the\n84\t   Affected Components section is a plain list without change/owner detail).\n85\t   Teams were taken from the architecture-context owning teams\n86\t   (`serving-runtimes.md`, `training-operator.md`, `distributed-workloads.md`);\n87\t   ACs were derived from the HLRs. The Documentation epic has no owning team in\n88\t   any source — assigned to RHOAI Serving as the strategy's primary owning\n89\t   team, with training content expected from the Distributed Workloads team.\n90\t2. **Hardware access (Open Question 1) is an availability precondition, not an\n91\t   epic or an AC**: procurement or partner-lab access for dedicated AMD\n92\t   Instinct nodes is granted by parties outside the strategy. It is recorded as\n93\t   a risk on E001 and E006; their ACs sit on the deliverables (the validated\n94\t   parity report, the demonstrated training jobs).\n95\t3. **External cadence timing (Open Question 2)**: AMD-controlled ROCm image\n96\t   release cadence has uncertain timing — tiered delivery applied (below). If\n97\t   cadence misaligns with release trains, E003/E006 fall back to the\n98\t   build-from-source strategy E002 selects.\n99\t4. **Priority inversion check (none found)**: the docs epic carries P1 despite\n100\t   HLR-4 being P2 — this is the mandated docs-authoring exception, not an\n101\t   inversion.\n102\t5. **Effort overage / re-split triggers**:\n103\t   - E001 (campaign) will likely exceed one sprint — it is kept as one logical\n104\t     investigation because splitting would fragment a single campaign\n105\t     harness. **Re-split trigger**: if the supported-model matrix exceeds a\n106\t     single campaign window, split into staged per-model-family campaign epics.\n107\t   - E003 may exceed one sprint if the cleared model set is large — kept as one\n108\t     logical change (single runtime surface). **Re-split trigger**: split by\n109\t     model family if per-model bring-up exceeds the estimated volume.\n110\t\n111\t## Tiered Delivery\n112\t\n113\tThe uncertain-timing external dependency is AMD's ROCm runtime image cadence\n114\t(Open Question 2). Partial value delivers without it:\n115\t\n116\t- **Tier 1 (independent, start immediately)**: E001 (risk-reduction hardware\n117\t  validation producing parity data), E002 (dependency assessment),\n118\t  E004 (pipeline readiness), E005 (amd.com/gpu quota handling).\n119\t- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated)**: E003 (gated by the campaign outcome and the\n120\t  cadence assessment), E006 (gated by the cadence assessment).\n121\t- Documentation (E007) follows all implementations per Rule 11.\n122\t\n123\t## Component Mapping Notes\n124\t\n125\t- All four affected components (KServe, Serving Runtimes, Training Operator,\n126\t  Distributed Workloads) have architecture-context files — all are in the\n127\t  platform; no onboarding chain is generated.\n128\t- **KServe is passive**: no code changes are required (the vLLM ROCm runtime\n129\t  runs under existing KServe InferenceService machinery, which is\n130\t  accelerator-agnostic). Validation through a KServe InferenceService is an\n131\t  acceptance criterion on E001 (same owning team, so no separate validation\n132\t  epic).\n133\t- **E002 component span**: the cadence assessment informs both the serving\n134\t  runtime (Serving Runtimes) and the PyTorch training worker images\n135\t  (Training Operator). It is assigned to Serving Runtimes as the most relevant\n136\t  canonical component (the P0 productization is its primary consumer); the\n137\t  span is noted here.\n138\t- The ROCm vLLM image is a new container image in the existing midstream repo\n139\t  (`opendatahub-io/vllm`) → repo-onboarding skipped; Konflux pipeline epic\n140\t  (E004) starts in parallel with the image work (Rule 20).\n141\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8d5f34aa3bcc6919","agent_id":"ab100ec37a64d7cbd","session_id":"ee31f95d-bee0-49c9-b